## 5. Quick unit test (so you trust the policies)

```java
@Test
void tryAllThenFail_attemptsEveryEntry_thenFails() {
    var calls = new CopyOnWriteArrayList<String>();
    var entries = List.of(new RedisEntry("a", "1"), new RedisEntry("b", "2"), new RedisEntry("c", "3"));

    Function<RedisEntry, Mono<Void>> action = e -> {
        calls.add(e.key());
        return e.key().equals("b") ? Mono.error(new RuntimeException("boom")) : Mono.empty();
    };

    StepVerifier.create(EntryFanOut.run(entries, action, 4, FailurePolicy.TRY_ALL_THEN_FAIL))
        .expectErrorSatisfies(t -> {
            assertThat(t).isInstanceOf(PartialFailureException.class);
            assertThat(((PartialFailureException) t).failedKeys()).containsExactly("b");
        })
        .verify();

    assertThat(calls).containsExactlyInAnyOrder("a", "b", "c");   // c was still attempted
}
```

---

### In one sentence

`EntryFanOut` = "for each `RedisEntry`, run this `Mono<Void>` action, at most *n* in parallel, and handle partial failures according to *policy*" — a single, tested place for that logic so `NationalCodeLookupHandler` (and any future handler) just calls `EntryFanOut.run(...)` instead of hand-rolling `flatMap`/`onErrorResume` chains.